  "verified_against": {
    "source": "release-manifest.json",
    "agrees": true,
    "note": "Hashes were downloaded from the published v5.39.0 release assets and hashed independently, then checked again by re-fetching the URL this pin itself names. Only the pegasus artifact changed: install.sh and the two builder scripts are byte-identical to v5.28.0 and earlier. v5.39.0 is the largest content change this distribution has inherited, and every part of it reaches DARQ under DARQ's own names. The engine now ships the top-level opencode.json key subagent_depth, which OpenCode defaults to 1 -- a value its own schema describes as preventing subagents from launching subagents. Because the engine never wrote that key, the may_delegate_to that four agents declared, and that the engine rendered faithfully into a task permission, was dead: the runtime refused the call before it ever consulted the permission. It is now shipped at 10, which is not a policy but a circuit breaker -- the policy is the sub-delegation criterion, where a level that does not compress is the wrong level to delegate at. Three phase-less specialists ship with it and arrive here as darq-explorer, darq-verifier and darq-implementer, derived mechanically with no rebrand.json entry, verified by running this distribution's own rename and transform over the three files. What separates each from its SDD namesake is one thing: what it returns. A phase agent returns a phase-shaped verdict; a specialist returns a result -- a finding, evidence, or what changed. That is why the verifier is safe to reach from an agent that writes: it is not a second authority, it is an instrument. The craft of exploring, verifying and implementing was extracted out of the three phase skills into shared files, so one criterion lives in one place and the phases keep only the artifact chain. The engine's readiness authority also got the scope it always had but never stated: sdd-verify is the sole authority for declaring that an SDD change may be archived, which is the gate it actually guards -- the previous wording read as authority over every change anywhere and pushed a person into the SDD flow merely to verify something. The orchestrator now launches independent delegations in one response instead of waiting for each in turn, names what delegating a trivial thing costs, and decides whether work needs SDD at all before routing it there. An adversarial review of the whole change ran the scenarios rather than reading the diff and found two guards that approved on a loose word instead of the fact, one of which did not protect the very fix it existed for; all three defects were closed before the release. This distribution changed nothing of its own for this pin beyond the alignment."
  }
